<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Al-Mundhir of Córdoba</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 842–888</span> · <a href="/centuries/9th-century">9th century</a> · Also known as al-Mundhir ibn Muhammad</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Al-Mundhir (c. 842–888) was the Umayyad emir of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cordoba">Córdoba</a> from 886 to 888, son of Muhammad I. He had commanded campaigns against rebels and the Christian north, and as emir he fought the rebel <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/umar-ibn-hafsun">Umar ibn Hafsun</a> in the south of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-andalus">al-Andalus</a>. He died during the siege of Bobastro, Ibn Hafsun's stronghold, and was succeeded by his brother Abd <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/allah">Allah</a>.</p></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Under <b>al-Mundhir</b> (886–888) and Abd Allah (888–912), Arab lords in Seville and Elvira also rebelled, and for a time the emirs controlled little more than Córdoba and its surroundings, though Abd Allah defeated Ibn Hafsun at Poley in 891.</blockquote><a href="/empire/emirate-of-cordoba">Emirate of Córdoba · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li></ul></section>
